        "biblical_teaching": "Paul directly rejects this idea in Romans 6:1 (\"Shall we continue in sin, that grace may abound?\") and Romans 6:15 (\"Shall we sin, because we are not under law, but under grace? May it never be!\"). James adds that \"faith, if it has no works, is dead in itself\" (James 2:17) and that \"faith apart from works is dead\" (James 2:26). Together these passages teach that genuine faith is not compatible with a deliberate, unchanged life of sin after professing belief.",
        "broader_teaching": "Rejecting grace as a license to keep sinning is one of the most broadly agreed-upon points across Christian traditions, sometimes discussed under labels like opposing \"cheap grace\" or antinomianism. Catholic, Orthodox, Reformed, and Wesleyan traditions all reject the idea that grace excuses continued sin, even as they differ on the precise relationship between faith and works in salvation.",
