        "biblical_teaching": "Paul teaches that believers \"are transformed into the same image from glory to glory\" (2 Corinthians 3:18), that God predestines believers \"to be conformed to the image of his Son\" (Romans 8:29), and that while the outward body decays, \"our inward man is renewed day by day\" (2 Corinthians 4:16). Colossians 3:10 adds that believers have \"put on the new man, who is being renewed in knowledge after the image of his Creator.\" Together these strongly support an ongoing, progressive transformation rather than a single completed event.",
        "broader_teaching": "This progressive transformation is commonly called sanctification. Reformed and evangelical traditions typically distinguish it from the once-for-all event of justification, Orthodox theology frames the same trajectory as theosis, or progressive growth into the likeness of God, and Catholic theology speaks of growth in sanctifying grace through the virtues and the sacraments. Despite differing vocabulary, all major traditions affirm ongoing transformation rather than a single static moment of change.",
