        "biblical_teaching": "Jesus asks pointedly, \"Why do you call me, 'Lord, Lord,' and don't do the things which I say?\" (Luke 6:46), and states, \"If you love me, keep my commandments\" (John 14:15) and \"If you keep my commandments, you will remain in my love\" (John 15:10). These passages tightly bind genuine love for and confession of Jesus to obedience, treating a profession of him as Lord without doing what he says as hollow. They support believing and following as inseparable in practice, though they describe obedience as flowing from love rather than declaring the two strictly identical.",
        "broader_teaching": "Protestant traditions have historically distinguished faith, meaning trust in Christ, from works, meaning obedience, while insisting that genuine faith always produces works, summarized in the idea that we are saved by faith alone but the faith that saves is never alone. Catholic and Orthodox theology likewise hold that living faith is inseparable from charity and obedience. Across traditions belief and following are treated as inseparably joined, even where they are conceptually distinguished as root and fruit.",
        "speaker_alignment": "The claim that belief and following \"can't be separated\" matches the retrieved passages well. The further claim that they are flatly \"the same thing\" simplifies a distinction, between trusting Christ and obeying him, that most traditions maintain even while agreeing the two never exist apart from one another in a genuine believer; this reads as sermonic emphasis rather than a substantive departure from the text.",
