        "biblical_teaching": "The retrieved passages affirm that God created the heavens and the earth (Genesis 1:1), that \"all things were made through him\" (John 1:3, Colossians 1:16), and that God himself is \"from everlasting to everlasting\" (Psalms 90:2). Scripture asserts creation as revealed fact; it does not present this as a philosophical syllogism about everything that begins to exist requiring a cause.",
        "broader_teaching": "This argument is a form of the cosmological argument, with roots in medieval philosophy and popularized in contemporary apologetics under the name \"kalam.\" It is one of several classical philosophical arguments, alongside Thomas Aquinas's five ways, that reason toward a creator's existence apart from direct appeal to scripture. Catholic theology, following Aquinas, has generally embraced such reasoned arguments as complementary to revealed faith, while some Reformed and Barthian streams have been more cautious about grounding faith in philosophical proofs rather than revelation.",
