        "biblical_teaching": "Scripture affirms that \"God is spirit\" (John 4:24), meaning he is not a material or physical being, and that \"he alone has immortality, dwelling in unapproachable light\" with \"eternal power\" (1 Timothy 6:16). Romans 1:20 speaks of God's \"everlasting power and divinity\" as perceivable through what he has made. These passages support describing God as immaterial and enormously powerful. The specific idea of God being \"spaceless\" is not directly addressed by the passages retrieved here.",
        "broader_teaching": "Classical Christian theology, shared broadly across Catholic, Orthodox, and Protestant traditions, has long described God as incorporeal, eternal, not bound by space, and all-powerful, developed through both scriptural reflection and philosophical theology such as the doctrines of divine simplicity and aseity in classical theism. This vocabulary overlaps significantly with, though it was not originally derived from, cosmological arguments like the one used here.",
