        "id": "c3",
        "claim_type": "doctrinal",
        "time": "0:30",
        "speakerText": "If there's no God, there's no objective standard for good and evil, just opinions.",
        "claim": "Without God, there is no objective standard for good and evil, only subjective opinions.",
        "cited_reference": null,
        "verdict": "partially_aligned",
        "verdict_basis": "broadly_held",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "biblical_teaching": "Scripture identifies God himself as the standard of goodness: \"Why do you call me good? No one is good except one, God\" (Mark 10:18; similarly Luke 18:19). It also describes God's own moral requirements, to \"act justly, love mercy, and walk humbly with your God\" (Micah 6:8) and to \"fear Yahweh... love him, and serve\" him (Deuteronomy 10:12). Psalms 14:1 links denying God with moral corruption: \"The fool has said in his heart, 'There is no God'... there is no one who does good.\" Together these ground goodness in God's own character rather than treating morality as free-floating.",
        "broader_teaching": "The idea that objective morality requires a transcendent grounding is a long-standing theme in Christian moral theology and natural law tradition, associated with thinkers like Aquinas and later popularized by apologists such as C.S. Lewis as \"the moral argument.\" It is affirmed broadly across Catholic, Orthodox, and Protestant thought, which locate the good in God's own character. As a philosophical claim about what atheism entails, however, it remains debated outside Christian tradition, including among some theists who hold that moral truths can be known through reason or natural law apart from an explicit doctrine of God.",
        "speaker_alignment": "The claim that God grounds objective morality tracks scripture's own identification of God as the sole standard of goodness and the source of what is required of humanity. The sharper claim that without God morality reduces to \"just opinions\" reflects a well-known philosophical argument that is broadly compatible with, but goes a step further than, what these specific passages state outright.",
