        "biblical_teaching": "1 John and 2 John directly confront people who ‘don’t confess that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh,’ naming that denial ‘the spirit of the Antichrist’ (1 John 4:2-3; 2 John 1:7), and 1 John 2:18 places this conflict explicitly within its own first-century moment: ‘even now many antichrists have arisen.’ These letters show the apostolic church already contending with an early, docetic-flavored error within the New Testament period itself.",
        "broader_teaching": "Scholars across traditions commonly read the Johannine epistles as responding to an early docetic or proto-Gnostic tendency, the idea that a truly divine being could not have taken on real flesh. Full-blown Gnostic systems with elaborate cosmologies crystallized later, in the second century, but the letters' internal polemic shows the church already contending with the seed ideas within living memory of the apostles, a reading essentially uncontested across mainstream Christian scholarship.",
