        "biblical_teaching": "1 John 4:1-3 instructs believers to ‘test the spirits, whether they are of God,’ and specifically identifies denial that ‘Jesus Christ has come in the flesh’ as ‘the spirit of the Antichrist’ — language 2 John 1:7 repeats almost verbatim against ‘many deceivers.’ The retrieved text centers on denial of Christ's real, fleshly incarnation rather than his bodily resurrection specifically, though the two are closely related historically.",
        "broader_teaching": "Christian tradition broadly reads this antichrist-spirit language as targeting docetism, the denial that the eternal Son truly possessed a real, material human body, a discomfort with matter that many early Gnostic-leaning teachers shared. The apostolic and patristic response insisted on the reality of Christ's incarnate life, death, and bodily resurrection together as one seamless claim, so a denial of real flesh in his earthly life and a denial of a real bodily resurrection were treated by the fathers as two sides of the same error rather than sharply separated matters, a point of broad ecumenical agreement.",
