        "biblical_teaching": "Scripture doesn't narrate the historical process by which the New Testament canon was recognized, but it does give the underlying principle the speaker is applying: Galatians 1:8-9 pronounces a curse on anyone — even 'an angel from heaven' — preaching a 'good news' contrary to the apostolic one, and 1 Thessalonians 5:21 instructs believers to 'test all things, hold firmly what is good.' These support rejecting a contradictory writing on doctrinal grounds, though they don't themselves describe Thomas or the history of canon formation.",
        "broader_teaching": "The historic doctrine of canonicity, developed gradually and affirmed similarly though not identically in scope by Catholic, Orthodox, and Protestant traditions, held that apostolic origin, widespread and consistent use in worship, and doctrinal consistency with received apostolic teaching were the tests by which a writing was recognized as scripture, not merely claimed to be so. On that standard, Thomas-type texts were examined by the early church and excluded, not overlooked; a popular modern framing of them as 'lost books' is generally considered misleading by scholars across the theological spectrum about how the canon actually formed.",
