        "biblical_teaching": "Genesis 1:27 grounds both sexes equally in the image of God at creation ('male and female he created them'), a point Jesus himself reaffirms in Mark 10:6 and Matthew 19:4. Galatians 3:28 extends this to salvation status directly: 'there is neither male nor female; for you are all one in Christ Jesus' — access to Christ does not run through changing one's sex.",
        "broader_teaching": "Historic Christian interpretation, across Catholic, Orthodox, and Protestant lines, reads the creation account as affirming full and equal human dignity for both sexes as bearers of the divine image, and reads texts like the Galatians passage as declaring equal standing and access to salvation in Christ for men and women alike, without requiring either sex to become the other. The Gospel of Thomas saying reflects a very different, Greek-influenced ascetic idea in which femaleness symbolizes the material or imperfect and maleness the spiritual or perfected, a framework foreign to the Hebrew creation theology underlying the canonical text.",
        "speaker_alignment": "The claim aligns well with these passages: nothing in the canonical text requires a woman to 'become male' to be saved or to enter God's kingdom, and Galatians 3:28 explicitly rules out that kind of hierarchy in Christ. The Gospel of Thomas saying she read reflects a very different framework in which spiritual perfection is coded as maleness, which these retrieved texts don't support.",
