        "biblical_teaching": "The retrieved passages consistently present 'the Father' as personal and knowable rather than an unknowable abstraction: Matthew 11:27 says the Father is revealed by the Son to those he chooses, Isaiah 63:16 names God directly ('You, Yahweh, are our Father'), and Matthew 6:9 and Matthew 23:9 have Jesus teaching his followers to address God intimately as 'Our Father' and 'your Father.' None of these describe the Father as an impersonal universal consciousness or as something one need not name.",
        "broader_teaching": "Classical Christian theology, whether Catholic, Orthodox, or Protestant, affirms that God is personal, self-revealing, and nameable, disclosing his name and character throughout scripture and supremely in Christ, which stands in contrast to pantheistic or nondual frameworks such as Advaita Vedanta or the popularized universal-consciousness language found in some contemporary spirituality teachers, where the divine is fundamentally impersonal and ultimately identical with all reality including the self. Historic Christian mysticism affirms intimate union with God but consistently stops short of erasing the creator-creature distinction the way strict nondualism does.",
