        "biblical_teaching": "2 Peter 1:20-21 grounds scripture's origin in the Holy Spirit rather than 'private interpretation,' and 2 Peter 3:16 warns that 'the ignorant and unsettled twist' hard sayings 'to their own destruction' the same way they mishandle 'the other Scriptures.' Together these ground the principle that a text's meaning shouldn't be dictated by the interpreter's own prior framework.",
        "broader_teaching": "The concern about reading one's own worldview into a text one is interpreting, commonly called eisegesis in contrast to exegesis, drawing meaning out of a text, is a basic hermeneutical principle recognized across Catholic, Orthodox, and Protestant scholarship, all of which emphasize attention to a text's original language, historical setting, and literary context as a check against importing foreign categories, even though the traditions differ on exactly how much interpretive authority church tradition itself should hold alongside the text.",
