        "biblical_teaching": "Deuteronomy 6:4, the Shema ('Yahweh is our God. Yahweh is one'), is the foundational statement of Israel's monotheism, and Isaiah 45:5 and 46:9 reinforce it repeatedly: 'I am Yahweh, and there is no one else... there is no other.' Mark 12:29 records Jesus himself quoting the Shema as the premise of 'the greatest' commandment.",
        "broader_teaching": "The strict monotheism of Israel's Shema, later affirmed by Jesus himself, is foundational and essentially undisputed across the entire Christian tradition. Trinitarian theology in Catholic, Orthodox, and Protestant thought alike is explicitly formulated as a way of affirming this same numerical oneness of God while also honoring the distinct scriptural testimony to Father, Son, and Spirit, and every historic Christian creed treats a pantheistic or monistic collapse of God into the universe as incompatible with this inherited Jewish monotheism.",
        "speaker_alignment": "Her claim that the source text is a monotheistic Jewish book rather than one teaching pantheistic monism is very strongly supported. The retrieved passages, including Jesus's own citation of the Shema, make Israel's exclusive monotheism about as clear and uncontested a biblical theme as exists, and nothing in this material suggests an undifferentiated, all-is-God framework.",
