        "biblical_teaching": "John 10:30 is the direct source: 'I and the Father are one,' echoed by the mutual-indwelling language of John 14:11 and John 10:38 ('the Father is in me, and I in the Father'). Notably, John 17:21 later uses the same 'oneness' language in prayer for believers ('that they may all be one... as you, Father, are in me and I in you'), which is relational and purposive language rather than a claim that distinct persons dissolve into a single undifferentiated consciousness.",
        "broader_teaching": "The classical Trinitarian reading, shared by Catholic, Orthodox, and Protestant theology, understands Father and Son as distinct persons united in one divine essence and will, a unity of relationship and nature rather than a literal identity or merging of consciousness. The same oneness language later used in prayer for believers is read as an analogical extension, believers being drawn into loving communion with God and one another, rather than proof that all such language means undifferentiated unity.",
