        "biblical_teaching": "John 10:33 and John 19:7 both record Jesus's accusers charging him with blasphemy and a capital offense precisely because, 'being a man,' he 'made himself God' or 'made himself the Son of God,' a charge Mark 14:64 repeats. Ezekiel 28:9 shows the same principle applied to a human ruler's pride: 'you are man, and not God,' rebuking self-deification as a grave sin.",
        "broader_teaching": "Historic Christian theology holds a strong distinction between Creator and creature, treating any creature's claim to divine identity as the gravest possible category error, while affirming that Jesus's own claims to divinity are true because he is, in classical Christian belief, the eternal Son incarnate rather than merely a creature. How other spiritual movements, including many strands loosely grouped as New Age, reframe self-deification as an aspirational or even highest goal is a matter of comparative religion and observation about those movements' internal teaching, not something the biblical text itself directly addresses point-by-point.",
