        "biblical_teaching": "Scripture treats marital infidelity with real seriousness: Jesus states that divorcing a spouse to marry another, apart from sexual immorality, itself constitutes adultery (Matthew 19:9; Mark 10:11-12; Luke 16:18), and Proverbs 6:32 warns that 'he who commits adultery with a woman is void of understanding... he who does it destroys his own soul.' Marriage itself is described as a union God joins together that people should not tear apart (Matthew 19:6; Mark 10:9). None of the retrieved passages speak specifically to legal or counseling costs, but the severity of harm scripture attaches to marital betrayal is well attested.",
        "broader_teaching": "Christian traditions uniformly treat adultery as a serious violation of the marriage covenant with real relational and spiritual consequences, though they differ on downstream questions such as whether infidelity provides biblical grounds for divorce and remarriage; most traditions read the Gospels' exception clause as at least one legitimate ground, while Catholic teaching maintains marriage's indissolubility and handles civil divorce differently from sacramental annulment. Pastoral and biblical-counseling literature broadly affirms that infidelity causes deep, often lasting relational and financial harm, consonant with wisdom literature's broader theme of folly's self-destructive consequences.",
        "speaker_alignment": "The speaker's illustration about the devastation caused by a wife's infidelity coheres with scripture's treatment of adultery as serious and self-destructive and of marriage as a union not meant to be broken. The specific financial details, attorney fees and counseling costs, are his own illustrative elaboration: realistic consequences consistent with, but not stated in, the biblical text. By casting the illustration exclusively as the wife 'running off,' he applies a general biblical principle about adultery's destructiveness through a one-sided anecdote, where the same texts condemn a husband's infidelity in equally direct terms.",
