        "id": "c4",
        "claim_type": "doctrinal",
        "time": "1:12",
        "speakerText": "Who sinned first, Adam or Eve? Eve. Who did God hold accountable first? Adam. Why? He's the leader.",
        "claim": "Even though Eve sinned first, God held Adam accountable first because Adam was the leader of the family.",
        "cited_reference": null,
        "verdict": "aligned",
        "verdict_basis": "lens_dependent",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "biblical_teaching": "Scripture affirms that \"Adam was formed first, then Eve\" and that \"Adam wasn't deceived, but the woman, being deceived, has fallen into disobedience\" (1 Timothy 2:13-14), and Paul traces the entrance of sin into the world specifically \"through one man\" (Romans 5:12), treating Adam as a representative head even though Eve ate first. 1 Corinthians 11:3 adds that \"the head of every man is Christ, and the head of the woman is man.\" Together these support Adam bearing a distinct, representative responsibility despite not sinning first, though none of these verses use the specific phrase \"held accountable first.\"",
        "broader_teaching": "This reflects the doctrine of Adam's \"federal headship,\" foundational to Reformed covenant theology and widely held across evangelical traditions, which reads Paul's letter to the Romans as making Adam the covenantal representative of humanity. Complementarian traditions extend this into present-day male leadership responsibility, while egalitarian and some Orthodox and Catholic theologians affirm Adam's representative role in principle without necessarily drawing a direct line to husbands bearing \"first line of responsibility\" for a wife's individual choices today.",
        "speaker_alignment": "The speaker's claim tracks a well-established biblical theme -- Adam's distinct representative accountability despite Eve sinning first -- grounded in 1 Timothy 2:13-14 and Romans 5:12 and reflected in 1 Corinthians 11:3's headship language. This is a mainstream, textually grounded position, not an invention, though \"he's the leader\" as the stated reason is the speaker's summary gloss rather than wording these verses use directly.",
