        "time": "0:00",
        "speakerText": "The Lord said, 'If you cry out for somebody who's in a sinful, unrepentant, rebellious state,' the Lord said, 'I will hear from heaven to deal with that person,' that is love.",
        "claim": "Crying out to God on behalf of someone in unrepentant sin, asking God to deal with them directly, is itself an act of love.",
        "cited_reference": null,
        "verdict": "tension",
        "verdict_basis": "lens_dependent",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "biblical_teaching": "The specific wording the speaker attributes to \"the Lord\" -- \"I will hear from heaven to deal with that person\" -- closely echoes 2 Chronicles 7:14 and 2 Chronicles 6:39, where God promises Solomon, \"if my people, who are called by my name, will humble themselves, pray, seek my face, and turn from their wicked ways; then I will hear from heaven, will forgive their sin, and will heal their land.\" In its actual context, that promise concerns corporate, national repentance tied to the temple, not a formula for praying over one unrepentant individual. Separately, 1 John 5:16 and James 5:16 do support interceding for a brother caught in sin: \"if anyone sees his brother sinning a sin not leading to death, he shall ask, and God will give him life\"; \"pray for one another, that you may be healed.\" So the underlying practice of praying for someone in sin is supported, but the specific first-person line the speaker puts in God's mouth borrows language from a different, corporate context rather than citing any single verse.",
        "broader_teaching": "Intercessory prayer for a sinning brother or sister is affirmed across essentially all Christian traditions, grounded in New Testament instruction to ask on behalf of a sinning believer and reaching back to Old Testament intercessory figures. Applying a corporate covenant promise given to national Israel to an individual's unrepentant sin is a common move in popular preaching, but more careful hermeneutics, emphasized in Reformed and many evangelical circles that stress reading texts in context, would distinguish promises to a nation from application to one person's situation today.",
        "speaker_alignment": "The speaker's core impulse -- that praying for someone caught in sin is love -- has real biblical grounding in 1 John 5:16 and James 5:16. But presenting the line \"I will hear from heaven to deal with that person\" as something \"the Lord said\" repurposes the distinctive language of a corporate covenant promise to Israel as a direct, individually applied quotation, which the retrieved passages don't actually support as a verbatim saying about praying against one unrepentant person.",
