        "biblical_teaching": "Scripture directly instructs believers to approach a sinning brother relationally and privately first: \"If your brother sins against you, go, show him his fault between you and him alone\" (Matthew 18:15), echoed in Luke 17:3's \"if your brother sins against you, rebuke him. If he repents, forgive him.\" Galatians 6:1 calls the spiritually mature to \"restore\" one caught in a fault \"in a spirit of gentleness,\" and Romans 12:18 urges believers, \"as much as it is up to you, be at peace with all men.\" On \"warfare\" specifically, the New Testament does use military imagery for the Christian life: \"the weapons of our warfare are not of the flesh, but mighty before God\" (2 Corinthians 10:4), and \"our wrestling is not against flesh and blood, but against the principalities... powers\" (Ephesians 6:12) -- meaning scripture doesn't reject \"warfare\" as a category outright, it redirects it away from fellow believers and toward spiritual forces.",
        "broader_teaching": "The priority of gentle, relational correction over combativeness toward fellow believers is universally affirmed in Christian ethics across traditions. The concept of spiritual warfare is likewise broadly accepted, though traditions differ -- Pentecostal and charismatic streams emphasize active spiritual warfare against demonic forces more than many Reformed or mainline traditions, which tend to read this language more corporately or ethically.",
        "speaker_alignment": "The speaker's call to reconciliation over division among \"brothers and sisters\" is well supported by Matthew 18:15, Luke 17:3, and Galatians 6:1. However, the flat statement that God is \"not calling us... to confrontations of warfare\" simplifies the picture: 2 Corinthians 10:4 and Ephesians 6:12 show the New Testament does frame the Christian life partly as warfare, just redirected away from fellow believers rather than eliminated as a category. The speaker's point about not fighting brothers and sisters stands, but \"not warfare\" as a blanket claim overstates it.",
