        "biblical_teaching": "1 Corinthians 5:5 instructs the church to \"deliver such a one to Satan for the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit may be saved in the day of the Lord Jesus\" -- directly describing handing an unrepentant, sinning believer over in a way intended for their ultimate salvation, in the specific case of a man in unrepentant sexual immorality within the Corinthian church. Similarly, 1 Timothy 1:20 describes Paul having \"delivered [Hymenaeus and Alexander] to Satan, that they might be taught not to blaspheme\" -- a corrective, not merely punitive, purpose. Both texts support the general concept the speaker describes, though in Paul's usage this appears to be a formal act of church discipline exercised by the apostle or congregation, not simply a private individual's prayer.",
        "broader_teaching": "Paul's instruction to hand an unrepentant believer over to Satan has generated substantial historic debate -- most traditions read it as referring to a formal act of church discipline (such as excommunication) carried out corporately, with \"destruction of the flesh\" variously read as physical affliction, exposure to the natural consequences of sin, or the pain of exclusion from the church, rather than an invitation for God to actively wreck someone's life. Pentecostal and charismatic traditions are more likely to treat this as license for individual imprecatory prayer against a sinning believer; other traditions read it more narrowly as tied to the apostolic authority and corporate discipline process described in that passage and in Jesus's own teaching on confronting a sinning believer.",
